## Analysis

**Definition.** $\limsup_{k\to\infty} B_k := \bigcap_{N=1}^{\infty}\bigcup_{k\ge N} B_k = \{\,x\in\mathbb{R}^n : x\in B_k \text{ for infinitely many } k\,\}$.

---

### Statement (a): Every point of $A$ belongs to infinitely many of the $B_k$.

This is **true by definition**, with no measure theory needed. The hypothesis $A\subseteq \limsup_{k\to\infty}B_k$ means precisely: for every $x\in A$, $x\in\limsup_{k\to\infty}B_k$, i.e. $x$ lies in infinitely many of the $B_k$. So (a) holds directly from the set-theoretic inclusion.

---

### The measure-theoretic hypotheses are inconsistent.

By the **first Borel–Cantelli lemma**: if $\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\mu(B_k)<\infty$, then
$$\mu\!\left(\limsup_{k\to\infty} B_k\right)=0.$$
Since $A\subseteq\limsup_{k\to\infty}B_k$, monotonicity of $\mu$ gives
$$\mu(A)\le \mu\!\left(\limsup_{k\to\infty}B_k\right)=0,$$
so $\mu(A)=0$. This **contradicts** the stated hypothesis $\mu(A)>0$.

Hence the three hypotheses
$$A\subseteq\limsup_{k\to\infty}B_k,\qquad \sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\mu(B_k)<\infty,\qquad \mu(A)>0$$
are **mutually inconsistent**: no probability measure and no choice of $A,B_1,B_2,\dots$ can satisfy all three simultaneously.

---

### Statement (b): Every point of $A$ belongs to all except possibly finitely many of the $B_k$.

Statement (b) asserts $A\subseteq\liminf_{k\to\infty}B_k$, which is strictly stronger than $A\subseteq\limsup_{k\to\infty}B_k$ and does **not** follow from the inclusion alone (one can have points in $\limsup\setminus\liminf$).

However, since the hypotheses are inconsistent, the set of configurations satisfying the premise is empty. Therefore the implication
$$\text{(hypotheses)}\;\Longrightarrow\;\text{(b)}$$
is **vacuously true** (ex falso quodlibet). The same applies to (a), though (a) is additionally true non-vacuously from the inclusion $A\subseteq\limsup B_k$ alone.

---

### Conclusion

- **(a) is true** — directly, by the definition of $\limsup_{k\to\infty}B_k$ and the inclusion $A\subseteq\limsup_{k\to\infty}B_k$.
- **(b) is vacuously true** — the hypotheses are mutually inconsistent (Borel–Cantelli forces $\mu(\limsup B_k)=0$, hence $\mu(A)=0$, contradicting $\mu(A)>0$), so the implication holds vacuously. Substantively, (b) does **not** follow from the inclusion $A\subseteq\limsup B_k$ in any consistent setting; it is "true" here only because the premise can never be realized.

Thus **both (a) and (b) are true**, with (a) being the meaningful content and (b) being vacuously true due to the contradictory hypotheses.

